# Performance

## Purpose

Keep generated code efficient under realistic catalog and checkout load without inventing an unavailable cache or broker.

## Scope

Apply to service algorithms, repository queries, database access, pagination, transaction duration, concurrency, and serialization.

## Mandatory Rules

- Do not add Redis, Caffeine, Kafka, or another performance technology; none is implemented in this repository.
- Avoid N+1 repository calls. Existing cart/order mapping loads products in loops; new code must batch-load referenced products or use a focused query when item counts can grow.
- Do not expose new unbounded `findAll()` endpoints. Use Spring Data `Pageable`/`Page` for products, users, orders, or other growing collections.
- Keep transactions short and free of network calls. Perform only the database work needed for one atomic business operation.
- Make checkout stock updates concurrency-safe with locking, versioning, or an atomic conditional update; validation before an unlocked save is insufficient.
- Use a collision-safe order-number strategy rather than `repository.count() + 1`.
- Select only required data for large reads and avoid serializing entities with accidental relationship graphs.
- Add or validate PostgreSQL indexes for new foreign-key filters, ownership queries, search predicates, and sort keys.
- Measure before adding caching or asynchronous execution, and document the measured bottleneck.

## Best Practices

- Build a `Map<Long, ProductEntity>` from one `findAllById` call when mapping cart/order items.
- Return summary DTOs for order lists and detailed items only on detail endpoints, matching the current service intent.
- Use database uniqueness and atomic updates as final concurrency guards.
- Keep `BigDecimal` calculations linear and reuse already-loaded product prices within a transaction.
- Add performance or concurrency tests for changes to checkout, bulk lists, or high-cardinality queries.
- Use explain plans only against representative PostgreSQL schema/data and record why an index is added.

## Anti Patterns

- Repository lookup inside a stream/loop for every row when IDs can be batched.
- Loading every product/user/order and filtering in Java.
- Holding a database transaction open while sending email, calling HTTP, or sleeping.
- Adding an in-memory cache that becomes inconsistent across application instances.
- “Fixing” overselling with only a synchronized Java method.
- Optimizing code with no identified query, allocation, or latency problem.

## Examples

```java
Map<Long, ProductEntity> productsById = productRepository
        .findAllById(productIds)
        .stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(ProductEntity::getProductId, Function.identity()));
```

Use a single batch query like this instead of one `findById` for each cart item.

## Checklist

- [ ] Query count is bounded and N+1 access is avoided.
- [ ] Growing list APIs are paginated.
- [ ] Transactions are short and concurrency-safe.
- [ ] Order identifiers cannot collide under concurrency.
- [ ] Required PostgreSQL indexes are present and justified.
- [ ] No unsupported cache, broker, or async system was invented.
- [ ] Performance-sensitive behavior has appropriate tests or measurements.
